diff --git a/examples/laya/README.md b/examples/laya/README.md
index 5723ebf..5e1af5e 100644
--- a/examples/laya/README.md
+++ b/examples/laya/README.md
@@ -301,3 +301,63 @@ Linux / macOS (same tree as the other examples):
 cmake -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DGGMLC_ENABLE_CUDA=ON   # or -DGGMLC_ENABLE_METAL=ON
 cmake --build build --target laya -j8
 ```
+## Adaptive memory profiles
+
+`--memory-profile auto` is the default. Supported Vulkan/Q8_0 graphs start with
+bounded weight streaming; after four successful observations the server can use
+resident weights for frequent or large requests when measured commit and VRAM
+headroom permit it. Unknown headroom prevents promotion. Other devices or
+unsupported graphs retain resident execution. Use explicit overrides when needed:
+
+```sh
+laya serve model.gguf --device vulkan:0 --max-batch 1 --memory-profile auto
+laya serve model.gguf --device vulkan:0 --max-batch 1 --memory-profile low-commit
+laya serve model.gguf --device vulkan:0 --max-batch 1 --memory-profile high-commit
+```
+
+`resident` and `balanced` alias `high-commit`; the legacy `--stream-weights`
+selects `low-commit`. Explicit low-commit rejects unsupported devices/graphs.
+Streaming Q8_0 matrices of at least 1 MiB share a fixed device slot, while small
+weights/constants stay resident. The full activation graph still needs memory.
+Source mappings can occupy reclaimable physical RAM; commit, RSS and VRAM are
+different measurements. Streaming increases transfer latency. CUDA capture is
+incompatible. Measure decision agreement and performance for the actual model.
+
+Auto uses exponentially weighted successful input tokens, forwards, sequence
+buckets and request-start intervals. The initial Q8_0/RTX2070 defaults are four
+observations, recent intervals <=30s or average tokens>=1024/forwards>=4/bucket>=512,
+with >=16 GiB available commit and >=4.75 GiB free device memory for promotion.
+Commit<=12 GiB, free VRAM<=0.75 GiB or unknown telemetry demotes immediately.
+Other switches observe a 60-second cooldown. These are starting thresholds, not
+universal admission guarantees. Available commit is sampled on Windows; other
+platforms currently report unknown commit and remain conservative in auto mode.
+
+The HTTP server unloads idle engines after 60 seconds by default. Override with
+`--idle-seconds N` (0 disables). Only model use refreshes that timer; health and
+catalog reads never reload models or train the adaptive policy. Idle unload
+resets usage history. The next decision pays a reload delay.
+
+### Cooperative vision/UI memory reservations
+
+`GET /health` reports `loaded`, `state` and `memory_management` including protocol
+version 1, reservation support, requested/effective profile, reason and usage
+averages. Compatible clients can yield all server engines before starting a
+heavy vision/browser job, including when high-commit was explicitly selected:
+
+```
+POST /v1/memory/reserve {"id":"job-unique-id","owner":"kritic-vision","ttl_seconds":360}
+POST /v1/memory/release {"id":"job-unique-id","owner":"kritic-vision"}
+```
+
+Control endpoints require a loopback peer without an Origin header and honor
+configured API authentication. IDs use ASCII letters/digits/-_. (max128), owners
+max64, TTL1..3600 seconds. A successful reserve acknowledges engine destruction;
+clients must wait for `reserved:true` before heavy work. New decisions return
+503 `memory_reserved` while any lease is active. Independent callers use unique
+IDs; releasing one cannot release another. Releasing is idempotent, expires on
+client failure, and retains a 60-second cancellation tombstone to reject late
+reserve requests. At most256 active/tombstoned IDs are retained. Release never
+warms a model. A client must complete/cancel its work before lease expiry.
+
+This cooperation releases Kev allocations only. It cannot create memory,
+change OS pagefiles or prevent unrelated processes from consuming headroom.
